<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Hasani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">10th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Hasani was a descendant of the Prophet's grandson al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-ibn-ali">Hasan ibn Ali</a> who took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in the 960s, around the time the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. He founded the line of Hasanid sharifs who governed the holy city, usually acknowledging whichever Muslim power ruled Egypt, and so began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharif-of-mecca">Sharifate of Mecca</a> that lasted until 1925.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn Husayn al-Hasani was a Hasanid, a descendant in the ninth generation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-ibn-ali">Hasan ibn Ali</a>. His sister was married to Akhu Muslim, a distinguished Alid sharif who rose in an unsuccessful revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-conquest-of-egypt">Fatimid conquest of Egypt</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a>, Ja'far came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, becoming the first emir of the Musawid dynasty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The year of his conquest is not known, and the sources disagree. Ibn Hazm placed it under the Ikhshidids in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and the historian al-Fasi narrowed it to 356–358 AH (967–969), since Ikhshidid influence in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> waned after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-misk-kafur">Abu al-Misk Kafur</a>, though he admitted that some chronicles have Ja'far naming Kafur in the Friday sermon. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> records that Ja'far had the sermon read in the name of the Fatimid caliph al-Mu'izz after al-Mu'izz conquered Egypt in 969.</p><p>It is uncertain whether Ja'far was a Fatimid partisan from the outset. Because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a> held much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, Fatimid suzerainty over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> was only established in 975, after the defeat of the second Qarmatian invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and peace between the two powers. When al-Mu'izz died later that year, Ja'far used his death, very likely with Qarmatian encouragement, as a pretext to renounce Fatimid suzerainty, but an army sent to cut off the city's grain supply quickly restored Fatimid control.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Ja'far's own line, the Ja'farid dynasty, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> until Shukr ibn Abi al-Futuh, with whom it ended in 1061. Ja'far is nonetheless counted as the founder of the long line of Hasanid sharifs of Mecca that followed, while the neighbouring holy city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> was frequently controlled by the rival Husaynids.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Hasani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 967 <b>Ja'far ibn Muhammad</b>, a descendant of al-Hasan ibn Ali, took control of Mecca as the Fatimid Caliphate rose in Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/state/sharifate-of-mecca">Sharifate of Mecca</a></li></ul></section>
